are the five lines
of code, four if you

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:33.180
don't include the require,
that you need to do this.

00:11:33.180 --> 00:11:36.460
So again, require
google/cloud/speech

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:40.890
will get an instance of a
client library, like a service.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.930
We tell Speech that this
is our local audio file.

00:11:43.930 --> 00:11:46.650
In this case, we have
a local audio.raw file.

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:50.390
And then all we do is we
call recognize on that file.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.190
Then if we print it out,
that gives us the transcript.

00:11:53.190 --> 00:11:54.870
It's really, really
powerful things

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.760
going on in just
a couple of lines

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.460
of really, really simple Ruby.

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:02.430
Translation, it's about
the exact same thing.

00:12:02.430 --> 00:12:05.700
Really powerful,
require, service,

00:12:05.700 --> 00:12:09.420
we're going to translate a
bit of text from one language

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:10.260
to another.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:12.930
How goes it, to
French, comment ca va?

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.170
Easy.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.210
BigQuery is really cool.

00:12:17.210 --> 00:12:20.340
Lets you query over ginormous
amounts of data really,

00:12:20.340 --> 00:12:21.214
really quickly.

00:12:21.214 --> 00:12:23.630
There are a bunch of blog posts
that some people have been

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:27.890
writing about, even processing
the entirety of the GitHub

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:29.122
public data set.

00:12:29.122 --> 00:12:30.830
And people have been
using BigQuery to do

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:31.985
really interesting things.

00:12:31.985 --> 00:12:33.526
You should definitely
Google for this

00:12:33.526 --> 00:12:37.730
if you haven't, like
processing every Ruby gem

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:41.210
file in every public
repository on GitHub.

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:44.240
And even parsing it to see what
libraries people are using.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.490
Supercool.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:49.280
One of my favorite things
that we made for our DSL

00:12:49.280 --> 00:12:54.320
here is when we were making
BigQuery tables for ourselves,

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:57.680
so we have data sets
and they have tables.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:13:00.560
When we were calling the
API and using the low level

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:04.310
API to make these things,
it felt a little bit wrong.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.940
As Rubyists, we're used
to defining schemas

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:10.110
in SQL databases.

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:13.010
So for anyone who has
done Rails, specifically

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:15.080
like Active Record,
or someone who

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:18.620
has used the SQL gem
to generate schemas,

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:21.520
this should look really,
really, really familiar to you.

00:13:21.520 --> 00:13:24.590
We use the idiomatic
DSL for creating schemas

00:13:24.590 --> 00:13:28.290
that you would anywhere else.

00:13:28.290 --> 00:13:30.480
And finally, one other
thing that I thought

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:32.760
was a cool idiom, just
because it's something

00:13:32.760 --> 00:13:35.790
that we didn't think of
initially that we were going

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:38.830
to add, but then as we
started using the library

00:13:38.830 --> 00:13:41.520
and talking to the service,
it made obvious sense.

00:13:41.520 --> 00:13:45.780
So for logging, logging
is a really great API.

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.390
You can call it to
send your log data to.

00:13:48.390 --> 00:13:51.540
Logs are associated with
particular Google Cloud

00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:55.950
Platform resources,
like a Google App Engine

00:13:55.950 --> 00:13:57.690
application in this instance.

00:13:57.690 --> 00:14:01.800
But what I find interesting
is actually what we did here.

00:14:01.800 --> 00:14:05.390
So if you're a Rubyist,
you've probably used

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:09.470
the Ruby standard library
logger class, right?

00:14:09.470 --> 00:14:10.640
We use it everywhere.

00:14:10.640 --> 00:14:13.580
Typically, if I made
a library, maybe you'd

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:16.220
be able to pass
in a Ruby logger.

00:14:16.220 --> 00:14:17.780
That's just a
standard Ruby logger.

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:19.460
They all have the same API.

00:14:19.460 --> 00:14:22.460
And then I would use that to
print out any of my messages.

00:14:22.460 --> 00:14:23.960
Well, when we were
using logging,

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:27.860
we thought, well, really, in my
application, that's all I want.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:29.960
I want a Ruby logger.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:34.490
So in this, if you
call logging.logger,

00:14:34.490 --> 00:14:36.140
what that actually
gives you back

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:39.320
is a Ruby logger that responds
to all the same methods

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:40.820
as a standard library one.

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:45.830
So of course, we are calling
logger.info Job started.

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.050
But all of those
different methods,

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:50.940
everything that a logger
supports, this does as well.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:53.030
So it can just be a
drop-in replacement.

00:14:53.030 --> 00:14:54.810
That makes it
really easy for you.

00:14:54.810 --> 00:14:57.870
These are the types of things
I love that we've been doing.

00:14:57.870 --> 00:15:00.270
Other products, click
through them, they're fun.

00:15:00.270 --> 00:15:02.060
I love this DSL.

00:15:02.060 --> 00:15:05.640
Cloud DNS, really cool that
you can manage your DNS records

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:06.660
from code.

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.070
But also just look at this.

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:12.040
I mean, just at a
quick glance, you

00:15:12.040 --> 00:15:15.860
can probably get a sense
of what this is doing.

00:15:15.860 --> 00:15:17.280
And of course, it
just, again, has

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:20.012
the simple require at the top.

00:15:20.012 --> 00:15:21.220
It needs to make the service.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:24.080
But then after that, it's just
doing a bunch of record updates

00:15:24.080 --> 00:15:25.700
within a zone.

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:26.490
Great DSL.

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:28.520
You can see we're
adding an A record.

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.200
We're removing a TXT record.

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.100
And we're placing
some MX records.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:35.230
Something that I know I
spent a lot of time in UIs,

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:38.540
and like my namespace, my
like DNS server's doing.

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:41.540
So knowing I can do it, and
do it this easily in code,

00:15:41.540 --> 00:15:44.420
it's really, really
cool in my opinion.

00:15:44.420 --> 00:15:46.790
Go check out the
machine learning APIs.

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:49.070
If you wanted to just
start playing with Vision.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:52.260
I wish I could honestly give
a full presentation on Vision.

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:55.460
It's that fun and that exciting.

00:15:55.460 --> 00:15:58.480
This little itsy bitsy
slide, small enough amount

00:15:58.480 --> 00:16:01.420
of code, that I can
make the text real big.

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:04.790
Require vision,
instantiate up the service.

00:16:04.790 --> 00:16:07.960
Tell it that we have
this local image file.

00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:10.750
And then all you
do, the magic line

00:16:10.750 --> 00:16:13.300
here is towards the
bottom, image.labels.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:18.100
All you have to do as a Ruby
developer is call image.labels,

00:16:18.100 --> 00:16:20.890
and that will give you
back a list of things

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:24.630
that the Vision API detected
and found in your image.

00:16:24.630 --> 00:16:28.430
So if it's an image of a
cat, maybe cat, mammal,

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:31.390
the type of cat, which I don't
even know but the Vision API

00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:32.290
does.

00:16:32.290 --> 00:16:34.230
Television, if it sees
it in the background.

00:16:34.230 --> 00:16:38.400
It's really, really
powerful and awesome.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:41.040
So that was a lot.

00:16:41.040 --> 00:16:42.810
Sorry for that, but
hopefully, you're

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:44.640
starting to see a pattern here.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:48.480
My goal is to allow you
guys to go back and have

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:50.520
a sense of what
the experience is

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:53.280
like using these libraries
so that you can get up

00:16:53.280 --> 00:16:55.860
and running really
quickly and easily.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:58.840
And also, remember,
the code is open source

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.330
and it's up on GitHub.

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:02.190
So issues are welcome.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:03.690
Feedback is welcome.

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:05.550
Definitely, thank
you to everyone out

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:08.339
there who has already
been a great contributor,

00:17:08.339 --> 00:17:10.050
has been giving
us great feedback.

00:17:10.050 --> 00:17:12.119
The community has been
really, really wonderful

00:17:12.119 --> 00:17:14.520
and really receptive for
this, and we appreciate it.

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:21.270
And if you remember one last
thing, when you get home,

00:17:21.270 --> 00:17:23.220
if you don't
remember any of this,

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:24.854
just remember go to our docs.

00:17:24.854 --> 00:17:27.270
This one page will get you up
and running with pretty much

00:17:27.270 --> 00:17:28.103
everything you need.

00:17:32.080 --> 00:17:35.060
For anyone who doesn't know,
this is a musical rest note.

00:17:35.060 --> 00:17:38.730
I use it for my water.

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:42.525
It's a quarter rest, for
anyone who doesn't read music.

00:17:45.550 --> 00:17:49.900
So before I show
you this next part,

00:17:49.900 --> 00:17:54.060
I want to just briefly
take you back in time.

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:57.810
I want to take you back to 2009.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:01.630
Now, 2009 was a pretty
interesting year for Ruby.

00:18:01.630 --> 00:18:03.790
Ruby version 1.9.1 was released.

00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:06.910
Now, this is important, because
it was the first stable version

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:08.770
of Ruby 1.9.

00:18:08.770 --> 00:18:10.930
And this brought us all
kinds of new things.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:15.010
This gave us an entirely
new Ruby interpreter, YARV,

00:18:15.010 --> 00:18:18.227
as opposed to the MRI that
we had been using previously.

00:18:18.227 --> 00:18:20.560
And there's lots of things
that, if you're newer to Ruby

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:23.080
and you didn't experience
the 1.8 series,

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:25.570
you might not know
came through this.

00:18:25.570 --> 00:18:28.400
Things like the new
hash syntax, where

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:30.880
you don't need to use a
hash rocket for anything.

00:18:30.880 --> 00:18:34.210
The arrow shorthand
for generating lambdas.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:35.380
Lots of cool things.

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:36.430
Basic object.

00:18:36.430 --> 00:18:39.010
All these things are
really, really cool.

00:18:39.010 --> 00:18:42.400
1.8.7 was what we had been
on for years at this time.

00:18:42.400 --> 00:18:46.180
And it was a big enough change
that it took a lot of us years

00:18:46.180 --> 00:18:49.540
and years and years, way past
'09, to actually fully migrate

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:50.040
our apps.

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:52.360
So it got us where we are today.

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:55.510
And interestingly, Rails 2.3.

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:58.720
Rails 2.3 was one of the most
exciting releases for me,

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:01.240
because it was the first
release of Rails that

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:03.970
was built entirely on Rack.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:05.620
So before that--
it's almost hard

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.690
to imagine if you're
familiar with Rack.

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:10.810
Before that, Rails had
its own layers for things

00:19:10.810 --> 00:19:14.650
like dealing with CGI, FastCGI,
and like all of the under

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:16.540
kind of servery bits.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:18.490
So this was super, super cool.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:22.540
So it also got to introduce
Rails Engines, Rails Metal.

00:19:22.540 --> 00:19:25.030
And just for a bit
of context, things

00:19:25.030 --> 00:19:27.580
like the Arel Engine
for Active Record,

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:28.960
for anyone who's familiar.

00:19:28.960 --> 00:19:31.450
Or even the bundler
integration that

00:19:31.450 --> 00:19:34.690
comes with Rails today, those
things weren't part of this.

00:19:34.690 --> 00:19:37.561
Those things came way after.

00:19:37.561 --> 00:19:38.850
Well, that's interesting.

00:19:38.850 --> 00:19:40.310
Cool little bit of history.

00:19:40.310 --> 00:19:42.810
But why am I interested in 2009?

00:19:42.810 --> 00:19:43.990
What does this matter?

00:19:43.990 --> 00:19:47.820
Well, it's actually just
interesting, really, to me

00:19:47.820 --> 00:19:52.500
because I gave a
talk back in 2009.

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:56.010
And it was the first time
I gave a talk on using

00:19:56.010 --> 00:19:58.890
Ruby with Google App Engine.

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:01.470
So really, it was my first
version of this talk.

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:05.100
It was my first talk on
Ruby on Google Cloud.

00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:08.070
And of course, at the
time, it was using JRuby.

00:20:08.070 --> 00:20:11.040
We didn't have Ruby running on
App Engine like we do today,

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.560
which we'll talk about more.

00:20:13.560 --> 00:20:16.990
But we did have Java,
so I could make it work.

00:20:16.990 --> 00:20:20.340
Now, when I was thinking
about today's talk,

00:20:20.340 --> 00:20:21.600
I remembered this.

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:23.490
And I actually went
back and I dug through

00:20:23.490 --> 00:20:24.960
and I found my old slides.

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:28.650
This is actually my old
title slide just remastered

00:20:28.650 --> 00:20:30.270
a little bit.

00:20:30.270 --> 00:20:32.980
And I found it interesting that
I did this eight years ago.

00:20:32.980 --> 00:20:35.310
My main thought was why?

00:20:35.310 --> 00:20:38.030
I'm talking to you
today about we're going

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:39.280
to look at Ruby on App Engine.

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:42.660
And I was wondering why, even
eight years ago, was that so

00:20:42.660 --> 00:20:45.110
exciting for me?

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:48.280
I answered that in the talk
I gave a number of years ago.

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:51.640
So here's the answer of why.

00:20:51.640 --> 00:20:55.625
Eight years ago, I started
playing with Ruby on App Engine

00:20:55.625 --> 00:21:00.500
and gave talks about it
because of the magic.

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:02.260
So magical.

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:04.360
Back when I learned
about App Engine,

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:08.740
it seemed like crazy
black magic how it worked.

00:21:08.740 --> 00:21:10.510
I liked to quote
something that--

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:13.480
I believe this code was on
the App Engine home page.

00:21:13.480 --> 00:21:17.800
And it is, "App Engine
uses multiple web servers

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:21.370
to run your application
and automatically adjusts

00:21:21.370 --> 00:21:25.810
the number of servers it's using
to handle requests reliably."

00:21:25.810 --> 00:21:28.290
At the time, that was crazy.

00:21:28.290 --> 00:21:29.430
That was amazing.

00:21:29.430 --> 00:21:33.310
I mean, my personal deployment
system in '09 was basically

00:21:33.310 --> 00:21:36.730
like a bunch of wires
taped together and managed

00:21:36.730 --> 00:21:40.990
by Capistrano scripts, which
in fairness, is kind of cool.

00:21:40.990 --> 00:21:44.050
I was really, really proud
of my Capistrano scripts.

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:46.870
But when it came to
things like scaling,

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:49.810
like what if all of a
sudden thousands of people

00:21:49.810 --> 00:21:52.810
hit my app tomorrow,
or my client's app?

00:21:52.810 --> 00:21:55.990
When I saw App Engine,
it seemed like magic.

00:21:55.990 --> 00:21:58.420
It seemed like the perfect
thing, especially when I was

00:21:58.420 --> 00:22:01.020
doing, like I was a consultant.

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:02.500
I'd make a Rails application.

00:22:02.500 --> 00:22:04.959
I'd want to hand it off
and kind of say, hey,

00:22:04.959 --> 00:22:06.250
you guys deal with the scaling.

00:22:06.250 --> 00:22:08.560
That takes too much time.

00:22:08.560 --> 00:22:10.490
I can't do that.

00:22:10.490 --> 00:22:12.820
Versus we could hand
off an App Engine app

00:22:12.820 --> 00:22:15.245
and say, yeah,
that'll keep running.

00:22:15.245 --> 00:22:16.620
That's going to
vertically scale.

00:22:16.620 --> 00:22:17.750
You'll be fine.

00:22:17.750 --> 00:22:20.400
Yeah, if you get a million
requests because of some big PR

00:22:20.400 --> 00:22:22.680
release or something
tomorrow, it'll be fine.

00:22:22.680 --> 00:22:25.020
So these are things that
really, really excited me

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:26.580
about App Engine then.

00:22:26.580 --> 00:22:29.500
And they still excite
me about it today.

00:22:29.500 --> 00:22:31.480
So just for interest,
and to compare it

00:22:31.480 --> 00:22:35.380
to today a little bit, even
though I was running on JRuby,

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:39.010
which I wasn't the
intended audience for App

00:22:39.010 --> 00:22:42.364
Engine at the time, it was
actually surprisingly easy.

00:22:42.364 --> 00:22:43.780
All you needed to
do back then was

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.440
sign up to try App
Engine for Java,

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:48.910
install all the SDK for
App Engine for Java,

00:22:48.910 --> 00:22:51.340
and you needed JRuby.

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:53.615
For local development,
they had a tool.

00:22:53.615 --> 00:22:54.115
It's great.

00:22:54.115 --> 00:22:57.400
It boot up in emulated
environment of App Engine.

00:22:57.400 --> 00:23:01.000
So you had a way that you can
run your application locally.

00:23:01.000 --> 00:23:03.210
And for deployment, one command.

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:04.960
One command and it'd
take your application

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:06.670
and it'd put it
up on appspot.com.

00:23:06.670 --> 00:23:09.620
Awesome.

00:23:09.620 --> 00:23:11.860
There were a couple
of restrictions

00:23:11.860 --> 00:23:15.610
that you had to deal with to
get all of this magic, though.

00:23:15.610 --> 00:23:18.700
There was no file write
access, I believe at all.

00:23:18.700 --> 00:23:21.250
Like even if you needed
to touch a temp file,

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:24.430
I'm pretty sure that was
basically, totally disabled,

00:23:24.430 --> 00:23:26.770
which made running a
number of frameworks

00:23:26.770 --> 00:23:28.270
and libraries
actually difficult,

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:31.030
which relied on saving
something real quick,

00:23:31.030 --> 00:23:33.520
maybe temporarily
to the file system.

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:35.830
And a big one that
I remember really

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:37.890
being challenging
for me was there

00:23:37.890 --> 00:23:39.970
was no relational database.

00:23:39.970 --> 00:23:44.380
So Datastore, which is
wonderful and scales amazingly,

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.810
it's just a completely
new concept.

00:23:46.810 --> 00:23:49.240
So to someone who is
used to building things

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:52.280
using relational SQL databases,
it really slowed me down

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:54.910
as I tried to learn
these new concepts.

00:23:54.910 --> 00:23:58.140
And there were a number
of other limitations too.

00:23:58.140 --> 00:24:00.400
But let's fast forward.

00:24:00.400 --> 00:24:03.130
So Ruby today for
Google Cloud Platform,

00:24:03.130 --> 00:24:06.600
there are actually a ton
of different options.

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:09.240
If you want, you can
use Compute Engine

00:24:09.240 --> 00:24:12.450
to have full control
over your environment.

00:24:12.450 --> 00:24:15.540
If you want to manage your
own Linux, Windows VMs,

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.650
and have complete
control, that's great

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:19.710
and we have that today.

00:24:19.710 --> 00:24:21.570
If you're already
using Docker, you

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:24.000
may want to take a look
at Container Engine.

00:24:24.000 --> 00:24:28.200
This is essentially Google's
hosted version of Kubernetes.

00:24:28.200 --> 00:24:30.060
And finally, of
course, if you just

00:24:30.060 --> 00:24:31.500
want to focus on
your application,

00:24:31.500 --> 00:24:35.130
like I did and still do,
then you can use App Engine.

00:24:35.130 --> 00:24:40.450
And it still has all of the
magic today that it had then.

00:24:40.450 --> 00:24:42.340
So App Engine is awesome.

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:44.740
If you all didn't
hear at the keynote

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:47.560
this morning, App Engine
Flexible Environment, which

00:24:47.560 --> 00:24:50.920
is what Ruby runs
on today, just GA'd

00:24:50.920 --> 00:24:54.640
this morning, which is super,
super exciting for our team.

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:57.790
And in addition to
Ruby, it runs a bunch

00:24:57.790 --> 00:24:59.170
of these other languages.

00:24:59.170 --> 00:25:03.580
So we GA'd a bunch of languages
on Google App Engine today.

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:04.570
And it's great.

00:25:04.570 --> 00:25:05.410
I love it.

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:08.050
So you can run a number
of the other languages

00:25:08.050 --> 00:25:11.020
if you're using them
in your shop as well.

00:25:11.020 --> 00:25:13.420
And of course, for
the Ruby Runtime,

00:25:13.420 --> 00:25:18.430
the thing that's important to me
for Ruby on this new App Engine

00:25:18.430 --> 00:25:21.010
is it still has all that magic.

00:25:21.010 --> 00:25:24.640
It still has everything I
loved about App Engine then

00:25:24.640 --> 00:25:26.500
and I love about it now.

00:25:26.500 --> 00:25:31.250
I make my application
and just let it go.

00:25:31.250 --> 00:25:34.310
And just kind of for fun,
just for a comparison,

00:25:34.310 --> 00:25:36.470
today, with App
Engine Flex, this

00:25:36.470 --> 00:25:37.670
is kind of how it compares.

00:25:37.670 --> 00:25:40.940
Today, you sign up
for Google Cloud

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:44.100
and you download and install
the Google Cloud SDK.

00:25:44.100 --> 00:25:45.770
So these are cloud-wide now.

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:49.820
And the Cloud SDK gives you all
kinds of tools and utilities

00:25:49.820 --> 00:25:54.800
for interacting with and
managing products across Cloud.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:57.350
And particularly
great is you can bring

00:25:57.350 --> 00:25:58.880
any Ruby version you want.

00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:01.541
So you're not locked
down to this JRuby.

00:26:01.541 --> 00:26:02.540
We'll get to this later.

00:26:02.540 --> 00:26:06.140
But any version of
Ruby you can run.

00:26:06.140 --> 00:26:08.390
For local development,
whereas before we

00:26:08.390 --> 00:26:12.440
ran a little emulator,
today, to locally develop

00:26:12.440 --> 00:26:16.350
your Rails or Ruby web
application, just Rail Server.

00:26:16.350 --> 00:26:19.672
Just use your local tools
as you use them today.

00:26:19.672 --> 00:26:20.880
Totally, completely the same.

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:24.120
There's nothing
App Engine specific

00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:26.190
in any of your Ruby
web applications.

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:31.350
Just vanilla, regular Ruby
app and we'll make it run.

00:26:31.350 --> 00:26:33.380
Now, if you are
asking, hey, Remi,

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:36.370
but I know your
production environment

00:26:36.370 --> 00:26:38.780
is going to be a little
bit different than my local

00:26:38.780 --> 00:26:40.160
development workstation.

00:26:40.160 --> 00:26:42.350
What if I want to
be 100% sure and I

00:26:42.350 --> 00:26:46.250
want to run my app in the
exact same environment?

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:49.580
Well, because App Engine Flex
is completely based on Docker,

00:26:49.580 --> 00:26:51.620
you can do that if you want to.

00:26:51.620 --> 00:26:54.986
You can use our same runtime and
you can Docker run it locally.

00:26:54.986 --> 00:26:56.610
We'll touch on that
a little bit later.

00:26:56.610 --> 00:26:59.090
But for the most part,
just do what you're doing.

00:26:59.090 --> 00:27:00.450
It's awesome.

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:03.110
And then for deploy,
the story is the same.

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:04.700
It's just as
magical and awesome.

00:27:04.700 --> 00:27:07.270
One command, gcloud app deploy.

00:27:07.270 --> 00:27:11.140
We'll take your application,
put it up on appspot.com,

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:14.200
or your custom domain.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:18.320
The restrictions, I got to
strike through all of these.

00:27:18.320 --> 00:27:21.350
And one thing that really
made it a little bit more

00:27:21.350 --> 00:27:25.130
challenging for me to use
App Engine eight years ago,

00:27:25.130 --> 00:27:28.921
the lack of a relational SQL
database, that's way different

00:27:28.921 --> 00:27:29.420
now.

00:27:29.420 --> 00:27:33.260
So today, we've got fully
managed SQL databases

00:27:33.260 --> 00:27:34.730
on Cloud SQL.

00:27:34.730 --> 00:27:38.690
And also, if you didn't hear
this, in addition to MySQL,

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:40.890
we now have Postgres available.

00:27:40.890 --> 00:27:43.070
And that will show up
in the Cloud Console

00:27:43.070 --> 00:27:45.590
to allow you to choose a
Postgres database real soon.

00:27:49.330 --> 00:27:51.760
Just so that you know, you
guys can see how to get up

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.700
and running, I just want to
walk through what you need.

00:27:54.700 --> 00:27:58.400
All you're going to need to
get your Ruby and Rails apps up

00:27:58.400 --> 00:27:59.950
on App Engine--

00:27:59.950 --> 00:28:01.210
you need a Google account.

00:28:01.210 --> 00:28:03.100
Sign up for a Google account.

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:04.630
You need to sign
in to Google Cloud.

00:28:04.630 --> 00:28:05.760
Get into the console.

00:28:05.760 --> 00:28:07.580
Sign into Google Console.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:09.520
You're going to need
to make a new project.

00:28:09.520 --> 00:28:11.380
If you haven't seen
in all the other talks

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.900
that you've seen at Next,
most of the resources

00:28:13.900 --> 00:28:16.050
in Google Cloud are
based around a project.

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:18.790
So here, whatever you
name your project,

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:24.190
your thing will deploy to
my-project.appspot.com.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:26.140
And you need the Cloud SDK.

00:28:26.140 --> 00:28:28.780
Hopefully, again, you've been
seeing this a lot through a lot

00:28:28.780 --> 00:28:30.380
of your talks today.

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.200
This gives us the
GCloud tool that we're

00:28:32.200 --> 00:28:35.410
going to be using
not only to deploy,

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:39.100
but also, it's the same
tool that we wanted earlier

00:28:39.100 --> 00:28:41.440
to authenticate our libraries.

00:28:41.440 --> 00:28:43.190
So this is really
useful to have.

00:28:43.190 --> 00:28:45.600
You're going to want it.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:49.380
Once you've got all that,
just from your applications

00:28:49.380 --> 00:28:51.720
directory, just cd
into your Rails app,

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.340
run glcoud app deploy.

00:28:53.340 --> 00:28:54.430
And that should do it.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:57.090
As soon as you run that from
your application directory,

00:28:57.090 --> 00:29:00.990
GCloud is going to say, oh, I
see this is a Ruby application.

00:29:00.990 --> 00:29:02.460
I know how to deploy that.

00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.020
Would you like me to continue?

00:29:04.020 --> 00:29:07.380
And for Rails applications
and most scenarios,

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:10.680
just keep hitting OK and
your app will deploy.

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:13.111
It will ask you for a
little boot command,

00:29:13.111 --> 00:29:14.610
so if you're doing
something custom,

00:29:14.610 --> 00:29:17.070
if you're using
Sinatra, it needs

00:29:17.070 --> 00:29:19.780
to know how to run your
application, where the file is.

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:22.710
But for the most part, just kick
off your command and it'll go.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:31.250
So that's awesome
and that's exciting.

00:29:31.250 --> 00:29:33.770
And I definitely recommend
that you all continue

00:29:33.770 --> 00:29:35.810
to look into App Engine Flex.

00:29:35.810 --> 00:29:38.480
But what I'm really
interested in talking to you

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:41.810
guys about today are
the things that we did

00:29:41.810 --> 00:29:43.910
and the things that the
Ruby Cloud team learned

00:29:43.910 --> 00:29:47.640
while developing this
to make this easier

00:29:47.640 --> 00:29:49.290
for ourselves and for you guys.

00:29:49.290 --> 00:29:52.200
Challenges we ran up
against when we were

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:54.400
trying to develop our own apps.

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:57.570
And the first thing everyone
needs to cover and deal

00:29:57.570 --> 00:29:59.760
with is dependencies.

00:29:59.760 --> 00:30:01.500
So of course, today,
you've probably

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.559
got all of your dependencies
listed in a gem file.

00:30:04.559 --> 00:30:05.100
That's great.

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:08.250
And when you deploy, we'll
bundle install that for you

00:30:08.250 --> 00:30:10.120
on the server.

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:14.010
We do bundle install it without
your development or test

00:30:14.010 --> 00:30:17.372
dependencies, which is
pretty typical and idiomatic.

00:30:17.372 --> 00:30:19.830
But that is something we ran
into when we were accidentally

00:30:19.830 --> 00:30:22.080
installing development
and dependencies when

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:24.070
we were playing around
with our initial images.

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:29.330
So those kind of things
that we've really done

00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:32.130
are now done and ready
with the runtime.

00:30:32.130 --> 00:30:35.130
And if, for some reason,
you don't want a jump file,

00:30:35.130 --> 00:30:38.130
and you don't want to use
bundler for your dependencies,

00:30:38.130 --> 00:30:39.990
you actually don't need do.

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:43.050
This 13-line app
just uses WEBrick,

00:30:43.050 --> 00:30:44.580
and it's one Ruby file.

00:30:44.580 --> 00:30:47.910
And this will run properly
on App Engine Flex

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:50.010
without doing any
dependencies or anything.

00:30:50.010 --> 00:30:52.470
It was actually a user
request that we got,

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:54.510
so thank you for your feedback.

00:30:54.510 --> 00:30:56.940
We've got it, and it
is really, really cool.

00:30:56.940 --> 00:31:00.450
You can GCloud app deploy
a single 13-line file

00:31:00.450 --> 00:31:02.280
without any other information.

00:31:02.280 --> 00:31:04.950
And this works.

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:07.470
When it comes to
dependencies, any Rubyists

00:31:07.470 --> 00:31:11.060
here have probably
seen this message.

00:31:11.060 --> 00:31:12.720
It's usually not
a good one to see.

00:31:12.720 --> 00:31:14.910
It usually means the next
couple of hours of my life

00:31:14.910 --> 00:31:20.290
might be dealing with local or
production development issues.

00:31:20.290 --> 00:31:23.940
So usually, this happens
when we're trying to deploy

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:26.970
and our production
environment is missing

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:28.740
some of the negative
dependencies that

00:31:28.740 --> 00:31:34.850
are required for gems that need
to compile native dependencies.

00:31:34.850 --> 00:31:38.560
For the most case, we should
have this handled for you.

00:31:38.560 --> 00:31:43.620
So our Ruby runtime installs
all of the dependencies needed

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:47.310
for those most common gems that
we run into this all the time

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:48.030
with.

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:51.540
Development header
for MySQL, or SQLite,

00:31:51.540 --> 00:31:54.810
or Postgres, those
ImageMagick dependencies

00:31:54.810 --> 00:31:56.640
that can be hard to track down.

00:31:56.640 --> 00:32:00.930
Things like XML2, XSLT, which
you usually need for Nokogiri.

00:32:00.930 --> 00:32:03.900
All these things, we've run
into the pain ourselves.

00:32:03.900 --> 00:32:06.780
And so we've made sure that
they're all there and all

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.770
handled for you.

00:32:08.770 --> 00:32:11.050
If you need to add
something custom,

00:32:11.050 --> 00:32:14.080
hopefully you don't, but if
there's a package that you're

00:32:14.080 --> 00:32:17.080
using that no other Ruby
developer would need,

00:32:17.080 --> 00:32:18.790
it's your own
package, you do really

00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:21.700
need to customize your
development environment.

00:32:21.700 --> 00:32:25.030
You can, because this
is all based on Docker.

00:32:25.030 --> 00:32:29.170
So if you run glcoud beta
app gen-config --custom,

00:32:29.170 --> 00:32:31.240
this will generate
your configuration

00:32:31.240 --> 00:32:34.720
to deploy a custom
Docker file setup.

00:32:34.720 --> 00:32:37.210
This will plop a Docker
file into your application

00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:38.390
directory.

00:32:38.390 --> 00:32:40.750
And what this is, this
is the same Docker file

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:44.870
that we use to deploy in
the background for you.

00:32:44.870 --> 00:32:48.400
So here you can see
it, but normally, we

00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:51.100
would actually just make
this Docker file for you.

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:52.600
And it's the exact
same Docker file.

00:32:52.600 --> 00:32:53.810
There's no difference.

00:32:53.810 --> 00:32:56.470
The only difference here is you
can see it, you can edit it,

00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:57.760
you can play with it.

00:32:57.760 --> 00:33:00.790
And there shouldn't be
anything hidden about it.

00:33:00.790 --> 00:33:02.800
Here you can see
the Docker file,

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:06.220
if you're familiar with Docker,
kind of inherits from this

00:33:06.220 --> 00:33:10.150
other image on Google
Cloud registry called

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:12.250
google-appengine/ruby:latest.

00:33:12.250 --> 00:33:15.100
And you could totally
find that on GitHub.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:18.350
That is available in our
Ruby Docker repository

00:33:18.350 --> 00:33:20.000
under Google Cloud Platform.

00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:22.036
So that's where it comes from.

00:33:22.036 --> 00:33:23.950
If you find bugs, you
want to file issues,

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:27.700
or anything like that, it's here
and it's totally transparent.

00:33:27.700 --> 00:33:31.240
You can see this is based off
of Debian 8 at the moment.

00:33:31.240 --> 00:33:33.700
And there's a lot of
useful comments throughout.

00:33:33.700 --> 00:33:36.760
So for these
dependencies, for example,

00:33:36.760 --> 00:33:40.090
we're keeping a list of
which aptitude packages

00:33:40.090 --> 00:33:42.370
we install and why
for which gems,

00:33:42.370 --> 00:33:45.040
so that we can keep track of
those things for ourselves

00:33:45.040 --> 00:33:50.650
and know why we added libcurl
or something like that.

00:33:50.650 --> 00:33:52.840
If you want to know more
about the Ruby runtime,

00:33:52.840 --> 00:33:56.830
including how to generate custom
things, how to do all that,

00:33:56.830 --> 00:33:59.430
I highly recommend you
check out the docs.

00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:02.650
Again, I'm pitching
the docs, but our docs

00:34:02.650 --> 00:34:05.800
for Ruby's flex runtime are
really great and have a lot.

00:34:05.800 --> 00:34:10.040
So for example, we just talked
about extending the runtime.

00:34:10.040 --> 00:34:11.127
You're back at your hotel.

00:34:11.127 --> 00:34:12.710
You're back home
after the conference.

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:13.570
You're exhausted.

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:15.949
You may not remember
everything from the talk.

00:34:15.949 --> 00:34:18.429
Again, just remember to
check out the docs here.

00:34:18.429 --> 00:34:21.070
So you'll be able to find
the docs for extending

00:34:21.070 --> 00:34:23.739
the runtime right there,
and other things that you'll

00:34:23.739 --> 00:34:25.480
probably need or
want when you're

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:27.520
using Ruby on App Engine.

00:34:27.520 --> 00:34:30.909
So like how do you choose a
different version of Ruby?

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:33.570
That's really important.

00:34:33.570 --> 00:34:37.929
To answer that, all we use
is a Ruby version file.

00:34:37.929 --> 00:34:41.460
So for any of you
guys who have used RVM

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:43.860
for managing your local
Ruby installations, which

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:46.530
is very, very common
these days, those programs

00:34:46.530 --> 00:34:48.760
understand and use
the Ruby version file.

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:51.270
So if you're using
rbenv, for example,

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:56.670
and you want to lock down your
app to use Ruby 2.1.0, whatever

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:59.580
it may be, you can
rbenv local 2.1.0,

00:34:59.580 --> 00:35:01.230
it will generate this file.

00:35:01.230 --> 00:35:03.820
And then when we Google Cloud,
when we gcloud app deploy,

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:06.540
play we read that in.

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:09.990
By default, we use one of
the latest stable versions

00:35:09.990 --> 00:35:12.390
of Ruby for you, so you
don't have to pick your own.

00:35:12.390 --> 00:35:14.220
It should be good.

00:35:14.220 --> 00:35:18.090
If you choose one of the
latest versions of stable Ruby

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:20.720
as your custom, that
should be pretty fast.

00:35:20.720 --> 00:35:22.650
We've got Debian
packages that we've

00:35:22.650 --> 00:35:24.690
precompiled those
as binary, so those

00:35:24.690 --> 00:35:26.580
install pretty quickly
when the Docker

00:35:26.580 --> 00:35:28.860
image has to build and deploy.

00:35:28.860 --> 00:35:32.230
And if not, if you're picking
some older or different Ruby

00:35:32.230 --> 00:35:36.060
runtime that we haven't kind
of pre-baked into the image,

00:35:36.060 --> 00:35:37.500
that's totally fine, too.

00:35:37.500 --> 00:35:40.620
We'll actually download and
compile that version of Ruby

00:35:40.620 --> 00:35:41.600
for you.

00:35:41.600 --> 00:35:44.280
And using ruby-build,
which again, is idiomatic.

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:47.990
All the tools that we, as
Rubyists, are used to using.

00:35:47.990 --> 00:35:49.590
Another thing that
came up, I mean,

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:51.547
of course, when we
made the runtime,

00:35:51.547 --> 00:35:53.630
wanted to make sure that
when you're app boots up,

00:35:53.630 --> 00:35:55.740
it's got the environment
variables that you

00:35:55.740 --> 00:35:56.880
would expect.

00:35:56.880 --> 00:35:59.310
RAILS_ENV=production, obvious.

00:35:59.310 --> 00:36:01.680
Some frameworks
rely on RACK_ENV,

00:36:01.680 --> 00:36:04.480
maybe a little bit less obvious.

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:08.950
Sinatra 2.0, which I have not
played with yet, uses APP_ENV.

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:11.080
So we got feedback
there and we added

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:14.680
that, so that whatever framework
you're using, hopefully,

00:36:14.680 --> 00:36:17.080
Ruby runtime will work
out of the box for you.

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:21.850
So those types of feedback
we love, love to get.

00:36:21.850 --> 00:36:23.640
So again, check out the docs.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:26.360
It's great.

00:36:26.360 --> 00:36:36.820
What I want to talk about next
is once you've got everything

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:39.910
up and running, everything
should be smooth sailing

00:36:39.910 --> 00:36:41.510
from here on out.

00:36:41.510 --> 00:36:43.990
You understand how to deploy.

00:36:43.990 --> 00:36:46.302
You can check out the
docs to customize things

00:36:46.302 --> 00:36:47.510
and to learn everything else.

00:36:47.510 --> 00:36:53.140
So try to figure out,
what do you do now?

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:55.450
So there's a couple of things
that I highly recommend

00:36:55.450 --> 00:36:57.550
you guys do when you check out.

00:36:57.550 --> 00:36:59.440
So first, we've been
putting together

00:36:59.440 --> 00:37:02.560
a list of articles
and how-tos for how

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:06.160
to use Ruby with and on
Google Cloud Platform.

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:09.400
So if you go to the
cloud.google.com/ruby,

00:37:09.400 --> 00:37:12.010
you'll find things like we've
got a quick start that just

00:37:12.010 --> 00:37:15.520
takes like a Hello World Sinatra
app and walks through the steps

00:37:15.520 --> 00:37:19.750
we did to just install the
SDK, gcloud app deploy it, go.

00:37:19.750 --> 00:37:22.200
It's got everything you
need there to do it.

00:37:22.200 --> 00:37:24.160
If you want to deploy
Rails, or if you

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:26.350
want to deploy
Rails with Cloud SQL

00:37:26.350 --> 00:37:29.140
and see how to get MySQL
or Postgres Cloud SQL

00:37:29.140 --> 00:37:32.186
kind of configured and hooked
up and get ready to go,

00:37:32.186 --> 00:37:33.310
you'll find all that there.

00:37:33.310 --> 00:37:34.990
So definitely check it out.

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:37.870
In addition, check out
these Ruby flex docs.

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:39.310
There's a lot there.

00:37:39.310 --> 00:37:41.950
And not just things that
are specific to Ruby.

00:37:41.950 --> 00:37:49.240
So for example, all these core
App Engine concepts, services,

00:37:49.240 --> 00:37:52.120
versions, how to
do something like

00:37:52.120 --> 00:37:55.746
do you know how to run something
like a Cron job on App Engine?

00:37:55.746 --> 00:37:57.370
Once you really start
using App Engine,

00:37:57.370 --> 00:38:00.230
you start to run into
all these questions.

00:38:00.230 --> 00:38:04.540
What if I need to SSH
into the actual image

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.000
and the actual container
that's running?

00:38:07.000 --> 00:38:08.830
Can I do that?

00:38:08.830 --> 00:38:10.960
All those things
are explained here.

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:12.600
And yes you can.

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:14.810
So definitely dig through here.

00:38:14.810 --> 00:38:17.660
And additionally,
the console itself.

00:38:17.660 --> 00:38:21.370
So it's really easy to
just download the CLI,

00:38:21.370 --> 00:38:24.880
deploy your app, see it's up and
running, and kind of walk away.

00:38:24.880 --> 00:38:27.760
But actually, I highly
recommend go ahead

00:38:27.760 --> 00:38:29.702
and also open it
up in the console.

00:38:29.702 --> 00:38:31.660
So Cloud Console is really
good to click around

00:38:31.660 --> 00:38:33.970
in because you'll
discover a lot.

00:38:33.970 --> 00:38:37.900
And for App Engine,
for instance, you'll

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:40.450
be able to look at all of
your running instances, all

00:38:40.450 --> 00:38:43.300
of your running
versions and services.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:46.000
And something that's
specific to App Engine,

00:38:46.000 --> 00:38:49.450
it's an App Engine concept, are
these services and versions.

00:38:49.450 --> 00:38:53.650
I'd feel bad, actually, sending
you guys home understanding

00:38:53.650 --> 00:38:55.720
how to deploy something
up onto App Engine

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:57.670
and not at least
understanding one

00:38:57.670 --> 00:38:59.890
of these additional concepts
of App Engine, which

00:38:59.890 --> 00:39:02.980
are the services and versions,
because they're unique

00:39:02.980 --> 00:39:05.650
and they're awesome.

00:39:05.650 --> 00:39:08.840
So when you gcloud app
deploy your application,

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:13.870
what we actually do is we deploy
up a new version of the app

00:39:13.870 --> 00:39:15.880
and we wait for it
to go ahead and be

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:18.730
ready to accept new requests.

00:39:18.730 --> 00:39:20.650
Traffic isn't going to it yet.

00:39:20.650 --> 00:39:24.010
Once it's ready to serve, we'll
redirect all of that traffic

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:27.880
to your new instance and we'll
roll down the other version.

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:28.600
Cool.

00:39:28.600 --> 00:39:31.910
Not that exciting,
but interesting.

00:39:31.910 --> 00:39:34.170
But you don't have
to do it that way.

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:36.920
So one of the things
that I do all the time,

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:38.720
and you should know
that you can do too,

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:41.720
is I'll deploy a version
and I'll tell it not

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:43.400
to redirect all that traffic.

00:39:43.400 --> 00:39:46.520
I can deploy my version and then
I can poke around and test it.

00:39:46.520 --> 00:39:48.680
Maybe you want to run
some sanity checks.

00:39:48.680 --> 00:39:50.990
Or you want to run a whole
regression suite on it

00:39:50.990 --> 00:39:55.350
before you OK it
for the new traffic.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:59.780
Additionally--
whoops, wrong button.

00:39:59.780 --> 00:40:02.390
Additionally, rather than
sending all of the traffic

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:04.670
there, you could
split the traffic.

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:06.710
So one of the things
you might want to do

00:40:06.710 --> 00:40:10.730
is with this new version,
start by deploying, sending

00:40:10.730 --> 00:40:12.470
1% of the traffic there.

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:13.560
Watch your error logs.

00:40:13.560 --> 00:40:15.440
Send 5% of the traffic there.

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:17.520
Watch your errors,
watch your logs.

00:40:17.520 --> 00:40:19.770
You can also use this
for anything you want.

00:40:19.770 --> 00:40:21.360
You can use it for AB testing.

00:40:21.360 --> 00:40:24.020
Deploy five different
versions of the application

00:40:24.020 --> 00:40:27.340
and see which one is performing
better, either with your users,

00:40:27.340 --> 00:40:29.300
or actually with some
performance metrics.

00:40:29.300 --> 00:40:30.800
It's really, really
powerful, and I

00:40:30.800 --> 00:40:33.000
want you guys to understand
that they're there.

00:40:33.000 --> 00:40:35.840
And additionally, services.

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:38.330
Because I didn't know
this initially, and now

00:40:38.330 --> 00:40:40.520
I love them and use
them all the time.

00:40:40.520 --> 00:40:43.940
When you deploy your Rails
app, or whatever it may be up,

00:40:43.940 --> 00:40:47.060
it gets deployed as
the default service.

00:40:47.060 --> 00:40:51.920
But actually, within one
App Engine application,

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:57.380
within one project, you can
deploy multiple different apps.

00:40:57.380 --> 00:41:00.200
So if I, for example, here's
one thing I use this for.

00:41:00.200 --> 00:41:04.740
I have a Rails backend, but
I also have a Node front end.

00:41:04.740 --> 00:41:06.950
And maybe a couple of
other microservices.

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:08.480
One manages authentication.

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:10.370
One manages something else.

00:41:10.370 --> 00:41:14.660
I could make five separate
App Engine applications.

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:17.000
I could make five
separate projects.

00:41:17.000 --> 00:41:18.980
So one would be
at x.appspot.com.

00:41:18.980 --> 00:41:20.142
One would be at y.

00:41:20.142 --> 00:41:21.350
And I could deploy all those.

00:41:21.350 --> 00:41:24.120
And that's fine, and
that has some benefits.

00:41:24.120 --> 00:41:27.284
But if it's just little
microservices and I want to,

00:41:27.284 --> 00:41:28.700
I can actually
deploy all of those

00:41:28.700 --> 00:41:30.602
together under the same project.

00:41:30.602 --> 00:41:32.060
So it can be really,
really useful.

00:41:35.120 --> 00:41:39.100
Again, we got back to hopefully
you're good at this point.

00:41:39.100 --> 00:41:40.160
Smooth sailing.

00:41:40.160 --> 00:41:42.140
You know where to
find some docs.

00:41:42.140 --> 00:41:44.774
You know where to go from here.

00:41:44.774 --> 00:41:50.400
But what if things don't go
so well, don't go as planned?

00:41:50.400 --> 00:41:53.790
You should also understand what
to do when there are problems.

00:41:53.790 --> 00:41:55.730
So you deploy this thing.

00:41:55.730 --> 00:41:57.380
It's been working great.

00:41:57.380 --> 00:41:59.510
But now you deploy and
you're getting 500s.

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:00.970
Things are exploding.

00:42:00.970 --> 00:42:01.830
What do you do?

00:42:01.830 --> 00:42:05.480
Where do you even go to look
to see what your application is

00:42:05.480 --> 00:42:07.250
doing, where it's breaking?

00:42:07.250 --> 00:42:08.750
So this is where
I'm going to review

00:42:08.750 --> 00:42:12.680
some the Google Stackdriver
tools real, real quick.

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:14.750
And this is interesting
because our team

00:42:14.750 --> 00:42:16.790
has been working most
recently on trying

00:42:16.790 --> 00:42:17.960
to make these awesome.

00:42:17.960 --> 00:42:20.390
Because we're at
the point where now

00:42:20.390 --> 00:42:23.180
we really want to be able to
get in and debug these tools

00:42:23.180 --> 00:42:27.070
and debug our apps and
understand what's going on.

00:42:27.070 --> 00:42:31.360
So if you just go ahead,
and after you deploy,

00:42:31.360 --> 00:42:32.640
check out logging.

00:42:32.640 --> 00:42:34.590
So after you deploy
your app, and here we'll

00:42:34.590 --> 00:42:39.570
just say, as an example, like
maybe our application logs

00:42:39.570 --> 00:42:41.670
something in case there's
an error condition,

00:42:41.670 --> 00:42:42.990
and then it explodes.

00:42:42.990 --> 00:42:45.750
Naive, but it works
for our purposes.

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:48.570
You'll run into this
horrible kind of evil page

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:50.520
that we all see as
Rails developers.

00:42:50.520 --> 00:42:52.360
Need to find out
what went wrong.

00:42:52.360 --> 00:42:56.060
Plop into Logging in
the Cloud Console.

00:42:56.060 --> 00:43:01.750
And then this is hard
to see, but here I'm

00:43:01.750 --> 00:43:05.350
doing like a full text
search to try and find

00:43:05.350 --> 00:43:07.285
this actual log and exception.

00:43:07.285 --> 00:43:09.640
But this was actually
the easiest way

00:43:09.640 --> 00:43:11.410
that I could find it.

00:43:11.410 --> 00:43:15.560
The logs print out
a whole lot of data.

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:18.630
So Stackdriver Logging
is really, really great,

00:43:18.630 --> 00:43:20.950
but it'll show you everything
their application sends

00:43:20.950 --> 00:43:21.760
to standard out.

00:43:21.760 --> 00:43:24.310
Everything that gets sent to
standard error, everything that

00:43:24.310 --> 00:43:26.230
gets written to the syslog.

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:29.620
You can also see like
the NGINX, like web logs.

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:31.870
And all of it in there
is really, really great,

00:43:31.870 --> 00:43:33.320
and it's got great filtering.

00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:35.500
You can filter it down
to a particular version

00:43:35.500 --> 00:43:37.210
or a particular service.

00:43:37.210 --> 00:43:40.180
But still, it can be
really hard to find things.

00:43:40.180 --> 00:43:43.250
And even when I do find
the line for my exception

00:43:43.250 --> 00:43:46.330
and I open it up and I try
and find my stack trace,

00:43:46.330 --> 00:43:47.950
this is my exception message.

00:43:47.950 --> 00:43:50.620
I know it's really hard
to see, but I open this up

00:43:50.620 --> 00:43:55.527
and it's not immediately clear
where I can find a stack trace.

00:43:55.527 --> 00:43:57.610
And now I'm frustrated
because my app is exploding

00:43:57.610 --> 00:44:02.140
and users are telling me
about it relentlessly.

00:44:02.140 --> 00:44:04.750
One of the things that
I see when I'm in here

00:44:04.750 --> 00:44:08.470
is you have the ability to drill
down these log messages based

00:44:08.470 --> 00:44:11.230
on severity, which
would be great,

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:14.980
but because by default,
Rails is just--

00:44:14.980 --> 00:44:17.980
like this just picking up on
standard out, standard error,

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:21.280
syslog, there's nothing
intelligent that we can get.

00:44:21.280 --> 00:44:24.580
We can't parse those
logs really reliably

00:44:24.580 --> 00:44:27.710
to figure out, oh,
this is an error.

00:44:27.710 --> 00:44:32.410
This is a debug
message or an info.

00:44:32.410 --> 00:44:34.630
And we have a solution.

00:44:34.630 --> 00:44:36.970
So we've been putting
together this awesome gem

00:44:36.970 --> 00:44:38.740
called Stackdriver.

00:44:38.740 --> 00:44:40.420
Actually, don't gem install it.

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:42.430
You're not going
to want to do that.

00:44:42.430 --> 00:44:44.470
Put it in your gem file.

00:44:44.470 --> 00:44:50.560
So in your Rails application,
put gem stackdriver, and that's

00:44:50.560 --> 00:44:52.500
actually all you need.

00:44:52.500 --> 00:44:55.480
Put Stackdriver in
there, redeploy it.

00:44:55.480 --> 00:44:58.300
And then your application
will automatically

00:44:58.300 --> 00:45:02.350
be sending logs and
sending trace information

00:45:02.350 --> 00:45:05.620
and sending errors that
could get reported, but only

00:45:05.620 --> 00:45:08.170
for the Stackdriver
services that you enable.

00:45:08.170 --> 00:45:10.840
So if you want this, go ahead.

00:45:10.840 --> 00:45:13.710
You're going to go
into Enabled APIs,

00:45:13.710 --> 00:45:15.430
and look up all the
Stackdriver ones.

00:45:15.430 --> 00:45:18.400
So if, for some reason, you
just want logs to get sent

00:45:18.400 --> 00:45:20.680
or error reports to get
sent, you can do that.

00:45:20.680 --> 00:45:22.270
So go in here.

00:45:22.270 --> 00:45:25.384
For me, I turn all these
on because they're great.

00:45:25.384 --> 00:45:27.550
Then once you've got that
deployed and you reproduce

00:45:27.550 --> 00:45:31.010
your error, this time--

00:45:31.010 --> 00:45:34.110
I was actually going to get
a screenshot of what logging

00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:36.570
looked like, so I logged
onto the Cloud Console

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:39.540
to go and say, hey, this
is what logs look like now.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:41.670
It's so much more beautiful.

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:44.250
But instead, when I logged
in the Cloud Console

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:47.940
after reproducing that error and
installing the Stackdriver gem,

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:51.840
this was staring me in the face
at the top of my Cloud Console.

00:45:51.840 --> 00:45:54.090
We print out this
beautiful tile.

00:45:54.090 --> 00:45:59.280
And it told me that my welcome
controller's index action just

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:00.550
had some runtime errors.

00:46:00.550 --> 00:46:02.970
And I can click to go
and see what's there.

00:46:02.970 --> 00:46:05.220
It'll send me into
Error Reporting,

00:46:05.220 --> 00:46:06.570
and boom, there it is.

00:46:06.570 --> 00:46:09.260
I can see a little mini
timeline of all my occurrences.

00:46:09.260 --> 00:46:12.810
If I want to, I can
click in to the exception

00:46:12.810 --> 00:46:14.220
to see what's there.

00:46:14.220 --> 00:46:16.440
And again, it's going to
be really hard to see here,

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:17.790
but for good reason.

00:46:17.790 --> 00:46:21.060
That's because it's
a big stack trace.

00:46:21.060 --> 00:46:26.160
So now this is way easier
and much better information.

00:46:26.160 --> 00:46:30.090
Similarly though, in logging
itself, the logs that get

00:46:30.090 --> 00:46:32.220
sent to logs, now that
I have the Stackdriver

00:46:32.220 --> 00:46:36.700
gem, all of a sudden the
severity is respected.

00:46:36.700 --> 00:46:40.330
All of a sudden the messages
are a lot more clear.

00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:41.500
Just runtime error.

00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:43.850
Just kaboom, error log message.

00:46:43.850 --> 00:46:45.850
So just having that
in there for logs

00:46:45.850 --> 00:46:47.920
is really, really
helpful as well.

00:46:47.920 --> 00:46:50.860
It'll be a lot easier for you
to filter just to your fatal

00:46:50.860 --> 00:46:53.740
or your error logs,
which I do a lot.

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:58.180
If your application is
getting slow, open up Trace.

00:46:58.180 --> 00:47:00.460
When you get in there,
if you've enabled it,

00:47:00.460 --> 00:47:03.550
you'll see this wonderful
graph of all of your requests

00:47:03.550 --> 00:47:05.780
that have been behaving badly.

00:47:05.780 --> 00:47:09.190
So all of your requests
that are taking, ooh, 500

00:47:09.190 --> 00:47:10.360
milliseconds or more.

00:47:10.360 --> 00:47:13.060
We've got one almost a second.

00:47:13.060 --> 00:47:16.810
And you can also see any that
particular URIs and drill down.

00:47:16.810 --> 00:47:19.490
But when you kind of
hover over one of these,

00:47:19.490 --> 00:47:24.190
you can click on it and
see what is taking so long.

00:47:24.190 --> 00:47:26.710
So obviously, this is
a Rails application,

00:47:26.710 --> 00:47:30.310
because you can see it's
showing the bits that

00:47:30.310 --> 00:47:33.370
are in Active Record,
how long that's taking,

00:47:33.370 --> 00:47:36.040
how long it's taking to
render the whole view.

00:47:36.040 --> 00:47:38.962
The view is obviously making
some SQL calls within it,

00:47:38.962 --> 00:47:41.170
so we can see all those
Active Record, Active Record,

00:47:41.170 --> 00:47:42.760
Active Record calls.

00:47:42.760 --> 00:47:45.880
And then if you want to go even
deeper, click on one of those.

00:47:45.880 --> 00:47:48.430
I mean, it's not helpful
to see, oh, Active Record

00:47:48.430 --> 00:47:49.900
is taking a long time.

00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:52.270
Click on one of those,
and for each one of those,

00:47:52.270 --> 00:47:55.270
you'll actually get to
see the exact chunk of SQL

00:47:55.270 --> 00:47:57.250
that's getting sent
to the database

00:47:57.250 --> 00:48:00.470
and how long that's taking.

00:48:00.470 --> 00:48:05.130
Stackdriver Debug I really,
really wanted to show today.

00:48:05.130 --> 00:48:07.590
This is kind of really
hot off the presses,

00:48:07.590 --> 00:48:09.890
except not off the presses yet.

00:48:09.890 --> 00:48:13.400
If you guys haven't checked out
Debug and seen it around Next,

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:16.410
check out the product, because
it's really, really powerful.

00:48:16.410 --> 00:48:18.580
I was just hanging out
with one of our engineers

00:48:18.580 --> 00:48:21.260
who was showing me some of
what we can do with Debug.

00:48:21.260 --> 00:48:23.060
And it's kind of what
you would expect.

00:48:23.060 --> 00:48:26.780
In your running Rails app,
you can set breakpoints,

00:48:26.780 --> 00:48:29.810
trigger the breakpoint,
and then see locals.

00:48:29.810 --> 00:48:32.690
Drill down and
put in expressions

00:48:32.690 --> 00:48:36.020
to see exactly what
variables were in what state.

00:48:36.020 --> 00:48:40.587
And even some really cool local
debugging, which seemed really,

00:48:40.587 --> 00:48:42.170
really magical and
I'm excited for it.

00:48:42.170 --> 00:48:45.160
So watch this space.

